.TH CPULIMIT 1 "2026-10-24" "cpulimit 1.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpulimit \- limit the CPU usage of a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpulimit
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.B \-p
.I PID
|
.B \-e
.I FILE
|
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpulimit
keeps the CPU use of a process, optionally with its descendants, near a given
percentage of one CPU. It does not change scheduling priorities. It alternates
.B SIGCONT
and
.B SIGSTOP
inside short chunks of time and adjusts the share of each chunk during which the
target may run from the CPU time it actually consumed.
.PP
Exactly one target must be given: a pid (\fB\-p\fR), an executable name
(\fB\-e\fR), or a command, which is started and limited
(\fBcpulimit \-l 50 \-\- make \-j4\fR). In command mode there is a single
.B cpulimit
process, the command is its child, and the exit status is the command's.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-limit =\fIN\fR
Allowed CPU, in percent of one CPU, from 0 to 100 times the number of CPUs
(for example 150 on a machine with two or more). Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid =\fIN\fR
Limit the process with this pid. Implies \fB\-z\fR.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-exe =\fIFILE\fR
Limit the process whose executable has this name or path. A name matches the
basename; a path matches the full path. The limiter itself is never selected.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-chunk\-size =\fIN\fR
Length of the control chunk in microseconds, 1 to 1000000 (default 100000).
Shorter chunks follow the limit more closely and cost more wake-ups.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-include\-children
Also limit the descendants of the target, found again on every cycle.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-minimum\-limited\-cpu =\fIM\fR
Processes using less than \fIM\fR of one CPU (0.05 means 5%) are left running
instead of being stopped. Default 0.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-lazy
Exit when there is no target, or when it terminates, instead of waiting for it.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Detach from the terminal and run in the background. Not valid with a command.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-pidfile =\fIFILE\fR
Write the daemon's pid to \fIFILE\fR (requires \fB\-d\fR). A pid file naming a
live process is refused; a stale one is replaced. It is removed on a normal exit.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-syslog
Also send events to syslog(3), facility \fBLOG_DAEMON\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-lock
Do not refuse a target that another cpulimit is already limiting.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-boost
Do not try to raise cpulimit's own scheduling priority.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print control statistics while limiting.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit with status 0.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text and exit with status 1 (as every release has).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The limited process was released after \fBSIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM\fR, \fBSIGHUP\fR
or \fBSIGQUIT\fR, \fB\-z\fR found no target, or \fB\-V\fR was given.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, a target that is already limited, or a target that is the limiter itself.
.TP
.B 2
The control loop failed (for instance an allocation or a system call).
.PP
In command mode the exit status of the command is returned; if it was killed by
a signal, the raw wait status is returned as before.
.SH SIGNALS
\fBSIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM\fR, \fBSIGHUP\fR and \fBSIGQUIT\fR make cpulimit send
\fBSIGCONT\fR to every process it has been limiting and exit. A signal that was
already ignored when cpulimit started (for example \fBSIGHUP\fR under
\fBnohup\fR(1)) stays ignored. \fBSIGPIPE\fR is ignored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cpulimit/
Directory of the per\-target lock files; when \fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR is unset,
.I /tmp/cpulimit\-<euid>/
is used. Each target has one file holding a \fBfcntl\fR(2) record lock. The lock
disappears with the process that holds it, even on \fBSIGKILL\fR.
.SH NOTES AND LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
If cpulimit is killed by \fBSIGKILL\fR (or by the OOM killer) while the target is
in its stop slice, the target stays stopped. No process can prevent this; send
it \fBSIGCONT\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Between reading a process and signalling it, its pid can in principle be
recycled; the window is one control cycle. Processes are identified by pid and
start time, which removes the much larger window of earlier versions.
.IP \(bu 2
Only Linux, macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD, NetBSD and Solaris/illumos have an iterator. NetBSD and Solaris/illumos are not yet tested on a real system.
Controlling processes of other users needs the privilege to signal them.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cpulimit \-l 50 \-p 1234
cpulimit \-l 25 \-i \-e ffmpeg
cpulimit \-d \-s \-P /run/cpulimit.pid \-l 30 \-e backup
cpulimit \-l 80 \-\- make \-j8
.fi
